  "analysis": "The filesystem is fuse-overlayfs with 0 bytes available (100% used), so apt cannot download 47MB of archives. I need to free up space or find an alternative. First, let me investigate what's consuming space and confirm whether writing is actually blocked, since the 'server' may just have a large but deletable dataset.",
  "plan": "1) Check disk usage of top-level directories to find large deletable content. 2) Test whether small writes succeed. 3) If a few hundred MB can be freed, retry apt install of git and openssh-server. If not, consider a Python-based approach (but git-over-SSH realistically requires the git binary, so freeing space is the priority).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /app /tmp /var /root /home /usr/share 2>/dev/null; echo '--- large files ---'; find / -xdev -type f -size +50M 2>/dev/null | head -20\n",
      "duration": 5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The overlay reports 0 bytes available and 955G used, yet du shows only ~65M across the checked directories and no +50M files. There's a large discrepancy — likely space is consumed elsewhere on the filesystem, or the overlay's available space is genuinely exhausted. I need to locate what's actually consuming space and test whether larger writes succeed.",
  "plan": "1) Get top-level directory sizes to find hidden large usage. 2) Test writing a moderately sized file to see if writes beyond a threshold fail. This determines whether apt (47MB download) is feasible or whether I must free space / find an alternative.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -25\n",
      "duration": 5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Strange situation: df reports the overlay backing filesystem is 100% full (0 available, 955G used), but du across the entire container finds only ~200M of actual files. The 955G is almost certainly in read-only lower/base layers counted by the overlay's df, not in the writable layer. The real question is whether new writes succeed. I should empirically test write capacity before deciding strategy.",
  "plan": "Test whether the filesystem can actually accept writes by creating progressively larger files in /tmp. If writes succeed, the df '0 available' is a false report and I just need to bypass apt's space check (e.g., apt-get download + dpkg -i). If writes genuinely fail, I'll need an alternative approach.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /tmp; for s in 1 10 50 100 300; do dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/test_$sM bs=1M count=$s 2>/dev/null && echo \"wrote $s MB OK\" || echo \"FAILED at $s MB\"; done; rm -f /tmp/test_*M; cd /\n",
      "duration": 3
    }
  ]
}

